  "query_preview": "/TUESDAY THROUGH FRIDAY/...\nIssued at 221 AM MDT Sat Oct 31 2026\n\nNext week starting on Tuesday and through Friday, we are\nforecast to be in a revolving upper-level trough and ridge\npattern over the area. On Tuesday, it is expected that the\ntrough system from the short-term period will slide east,\nbringing in a cold front, and putting us into northwesterly flow\naloft as a de-amplifying ridge follows in from the souther\nRockies on Wednesday. Once the ridge completely de-amplifies and\nflattens out over us, it would likely put us into zonal flow on\nThursday. After this, a possible additional ridge could form in\nto the south and amplify to the north on Friday. With this being\nsaid, there is some marginal spread of uncertainty within the\noverall evolution of the pattern. While zonal flow is favored,\nGEFS ensemble spread shows some variability of the small trough\non Thursday that could lead into slightly northwesterly flow\nbefore the ridge arrives Friday. There is low confidence (30%)\nthat upper-level northwesterly flow will occur earlier on\nThursday.\n\nAfternoon temperatures for this long-term period continue to be\nfairly consistent with highs forecast to be in the 50s. However\non Wednesday and onward, near freezing morning temperatures are\nexpected to occur in the Tri-State border area. With the spread\nof the overall pattern later in the week, there is low\nconfidence (20%) that temperatures could drop a few degrees into\nbelow freezing over the entire County Warning Area (CWA) if the\npattern becomes northwesterly. Otherwise, morning lows will\nlikely be in the 30s and slightly above freezing.\n\nOn Tuesday, showers and strong winds are expected to end once\nthe initial trough system passes by. Showers are likely to exit\nout of the area before the mid afternoon hours with winds from\nthe northwest decreasing from 30 mph to 10 mph. For the rest of\nthe period, we can expect the winds to be calm and shift a\ncouple of times with the changing pattern. Clouds should remain\nrelatively low before possibly building back up on Friday.",
